INT. LORNE HOUSE - LIBRARY - NIGHT

A fire dying in a marble hearth. Four chairs in a half-circle. A decanter, four glasses, a ledger left open on the reading stand.

AUGUSTIN LORNE, 60s, shrunken in a velvet smoking jacket, sits nearest the fire. His niece BEATRIX, 30s, stands behind his chair. CEDRIC MOWBRAY, 40s, the family solicitor, occupies a leather wingback. HESTER QUAYLE, 50s, the housekeeper, hovers at the door with her hands folded.

Beatrix pours for the men and does not pour for herself.

AUGUSTIN
Cedric, the clause.

CEDRIC
The clause is clear.

BEATRIX
Clear to whom?

CEDRIC
To anyone who reads.

HESTER
I read it twice.

AUGUSTIN
Of course you did, Hester.

BEATRIX
She was in the room when you signed.

HESTER
I was in the hall.

BEATRIX
Then you heard it through the door.

HESTER
The door was open.

CEDRIC
Doors are often open in this house.

AUGUSTIN
Not the study door.

BEATRIX
No. The study door is always locked.

HESTER
It was open on Thursday.

CEDRIC
Thursday?

HESTER
When the ledger went missing.

AUGUSTIN
(slowly)
The ledger is here.

BEATRIX
Because someone put it back.

CEDRIC
Or because it was never gone.

HESTER
Mr. Augustin, I told you I saw it on Thursday. Pages torn. Four of them.

AUGUSTIN
Four.

BEATRIX
There are four of us.

A silence. The fire pops.

CEDRIC
Surely you are not suggesting --

BEATRIX
I'm suggesting nothing. I'm counting.

AUGUSTIN
Hester. The pages. Where?

HESTER
In the second drawer of the hall cabinet. Under the umbrellas.

CEDRIC
You looked?

HESTER
I clean.

AUGUSTIN
Beatrix, fetch them.

Beatrix does not move. Cedric slowly sets his glass on the table, Hester's eyes find the carpet, and Augustin lifts the poker from its stand and holds it across his knees.

AUGUSTIN (CONT'D)
Whoever fetches them first.
